  "messages": [
    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Choose one next planning step using the local decision below. Return exactly one listed statement and stop. No other planning choices have been supplied.\n{\"context\": \"Every accepted item needs a label with its identifier, description, known limitations, and whether accessories are included; a blank template is available.\", \"decision\": \"How should useful item descriptions be prepared?\", \"goal\": \"Help usable household goods reach interested neighbors while keeping contributions, claims, and final collection understandable.\", \"problem\": \"Neighbors are organizing a one-day fair for clean, usable household goods that people no longer need. Giving and taking are both optional, and no payment or matching contribution is required. Volunteers have intake space, category tables, reserve shelves, a holding area, carts, and simple item records. Contributors choose in advance whether unclaimed goods should be returned during the closing collection period or donated through an arranged pickup.\"}\nCANDIDATE STATEMENTS:\nIntake volunteers ask contributors for item details and write the labels during acceptance. The same person translates the conversation into the standard fields, reducing differences in how descriptions and known limitations are presented.\nContributors describe accepted items to a labeling volunteer after the condition check, and the volunteer attaches completed labels. Description work has its own focused handover, keeping the inspection station available for the next contribution.\nContributors complete the item-label template, and intake volunteers confirm it before attaching the label to accepted goods. Firsthand descriptions are captured early while a brief review keeps the displayed information consistent across contributions."
    }
  ],
